          "secNumber": "5.2",
          "heading": "Non-Uniform Random Variate Generation: Inverse Transform and Box–Muller Methods",
          "content": "Once a reliable stream of uniform variates $U \\sim \\text{Uniform}(0, 1)$ is established, computational statistics requires transformations to generate random variables from arbitrary continuous and discrete target probability distributions (Gaussian, Exponential, Gamma, Beta, Poisson).\n\n### The Probability Integral Transform and the Inversion Method\n\nThe most general and mathematically elegant method for generating non-uniform random variates is the **Inverse Transform Method**, founded upon the **Probability Integral Transform**.\n\n#### The Fundamental Theorem:\nLet $X$ be a continuous random variable with strictly increasing cumulative distribution function (CDF) $F(x) = \\mathbb{P}(X \\le x)$. Define the generalized inverse (quantile function):\n\n$$F^{-1}(u) = \\inf \\{ x : F(x) \\ge u \\}, \\quad u \\in (0, 1)$$\n\nIf $U \\sim \\text{Uniform}(0, 1)$, then the transformed random variable:\n\n$$X = F^{-1}(U)$$\n\nfollows the exact distribution $F(x)$.\n\n#### Mathematical Proof:\nFor any $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$:\n$$\\mathbb{P}(X \\le x) = \\mathbb{P}(F^{-1}(U) \\le x)$$\nBecause $F(x)$ is non-decreasing, the event $\\{F^{-1}(U) \\le x\\}$ is identical to the event $\\{U \\le F(x)\\}$:\n$$\\mathbb{P}(F^{-1}(U) \\le x) = \\mathbb{P}(U \\le F(x))$$\nSince $U$ is uniformly distributed on $(0, 1)$, $\\mathbb{P}(U \\le u) = u$ for all $u \\in [0, 1]$. Setting $u = F(x)$:\n$$\\mathbb{P}(U \\le F(x)) = F(x)$$\nTherefore, $\\mathbb{P}(X \\le x) = F(x)$, proving that $X$ has CDF $F(x)$.\n\n#### Example: Exponential Random Variate Generation\nThe Exponential distribution with rate parameter $\\lambda > 0$ has CDF:\n$$F(x) = 1 - e^{-\\lambda x}, \\quad x \\ge 0$$\nTo find the quantile function, invert the equation $u = 1 - e^{-\\lambda x}$:\n$$e^{-\\lambda x} = 1 - u \\implies -\\lambda x = \\ln(1 - u) \\implies x = -\\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\ln(1 - u)$$\nBecause $U \\sim \\text{Uniform}(0, 1)$ implies that $1 - U \\stackrel{d}{=} U$, we can generate exponential variates via:\n$$X = -\\frac{1}{\\lambda} \\ln(U)$$\n\n#### Discrete Inversion and the Walker Alias Method\nFor discrete distributions taking values $k \\in \\{1, \\dots, K\\}$ with probabilities $p_k$, inversion evaluates $\\sum_{j=1}^{k-1} p_j < U \\le \\sum_{j=1}^k p_j$. Naive linear search requires $\\mathcal{O}(K)$ comparisons per variate. Binary search reduces this to $\\mathcal{O}(\\log K)$.\n\nFor high-throughput discrete sampling from static distributions, Alastair Walker (1977) formulated the **Alias Method**. The alias method decomposes any $K$-category discrete distribution into an equal mixture of $K$ two-point distributions in $\\mathcal{O}(K)$ setup time. Once constructed, generating each discrete random variate requires only **one uniform draw and one table lookup, executing in strictly $\\mathcal{O}(1)$ constant time**!\n\n### The Box–Muller Transform for Bivariate Normal Generation\n\nWhile the inverse transform method is universal, inverting the Gaussian CDF $\\Phi(x)$ requires evaluating the non-elementary error function ($\\text{erf}$), which involves numerical polynomial approximations.\n\nIn 1958, George Box and Mervin Muller derived an exact, closed-form algebraic transformation to generate independent standard normal variates in pairs.\n\n#### The Box–Muller Equations:\nLet $U_1, U_2 \\sim \\text{i.i.d. Uniform}(0, 1)$. Define two transformed variables:\n\n$$Z_1 = \\sqrt{-2 \\ln U_1} \\cos(2\\pi U_2)$$\n\n$$Z_2 = \\sqrt{-2 \\ln U_1} \\sin(2\\pi U_2)$$\n\nThen $Z_1$ and $Z_2$ are strictly **independent standard normal random variables**:\n$$Z_1, Z_2 \\sim \\text{i.i.d. } \\mathcal{N}(0, 1)$$\n\n#### Geometric Intuition:\nConsider a bivariate standard normal distribution $(Z_1, Z_2) \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$ with joint density:\n$$f(z_1, z_2) = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} e^{-(z_1^2 + z_2^2) / 2}$$\nConverting to polar coordinates $(R, \\Theta)$ where $z_1 = R \\cos\\Theta$ and $z_2 = R \\sin\\Theta$:\n- The angle $\\Theta$ is uniformly distributed on $[0, 2\\pi)$: $\\Theta = 2\\pi U_2$.\n- The squared radius $R^2 = Z_1^2 + Z_2^2$ is the sum of two squared standard normals, following a Chi-square distribution with 2 degrees of freedom: $\\chi_2^2 \\equiv \\text{Exponential}(\\lambda = 1/2)$.\n- Generating $R^2 \\sim \\text{Exponential}(1/2)$ via inversion yields:\n  $$R^2 = -2 \\ln U_1 \\implies R = \\sqrt{-2 \\ln U_1}$$\nSubstituting $R$ and $\\Theta$ back into Cartesian coordinates recovers the Box–Muller equations.\n\n#### The Marsaglia Polar Method:\nEvaluating transcendental trigonometric functions ($\\cos$ and $\\sin$) in Box–Muller is computationally expensive. To optimize execution, George Marsaglia developed the **Polar Rejection Method**:\n1. Draw $V_1, V_2 \\sim \\text{Uniform}(-1, 1)$ uniformly from the square $[-1, 1]^2$.\n2. Compute $S = V_1^2 + V_2^2$.\n3. If $S \\ge 1$ or $S = 0$, reject the pair and draw again (acceptance probability $\\pi / 4 \\approx 78.5\\%$).\n4. Compute:\n   $$Z_1 = V_1 \\sqrt{\\frac{-2 \\ln S}{S}}, \\quad Z_2 = V_2 \\sqrt{\\frac{-2 \\ln S}{S}}$$\nBecause $\\cos\\Theta = V_1 / \\sqrt{S}$ and $\\sin\\Theta = V_2 / \\sqrt{S}$, this method replaces expensive trigonometric calls with fast algebraic operations.\n\n---",
